          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">12. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          For most small-boat anglers the Minn Kota Endura C2 55 is the trolling motor to buy: a 12V hand-steer transom motor from the brand with the deepest parts and dealer network, listed at $259.99. If you fish from a bass or pontoon boat and want the motor to hold you on a spot, a GPS bow mount like the Minn Kota Terrova QUEST or MotorGuide Xi5 is the real upgrade. Size by loaded boat weight and shaft length first, and the brand second.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            A trolling motor has a narrow job: move a fishing boat quietly and slowly, and hold it where you want it, without the noise and wake of an outboard. That sounds simple, but the wrong motor fails in predictable ways. Too little thrust and the boat stalls in wind. Too short a shaft and the prop sucks air every time a wave lifts the bow. The wrong voltage and you are buying batteries you did not plan for. Each of those is a sizing mistake, not a brand mistake.
          </p>

          <p>
            We built this list from manufacturer specifications, retailer listings and published reviews. We did not lab-test these motors, and where sources disagree on shaft length, amp draw, weight or warranty, we say so on the product card rather than picking the more flattering number. Several of these motors are sold in multiple shaft lengths and bundles under one name, which is the single most common way buyers end up with the wrong one.
          </p>

          <p>
            We chose one pick for each kind of boat and buyer: a dependable transom motor for small freshwater boats, a GPS bow mount for bass and pontoon boats, a brushless premium option, a saltwater bow mount on a 12V system, a budget saltwater transom motor and a short-shaft motor for kayaks and canoes. The right one for you depends on your boat far more than on the brand.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="thrust" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How Much Thrust You Need
          </h2>

          <p>
            Thrust is measured in pounds, and the common rule of thumb is at least 2 lb of thrust for every 100 lb of fully loaded boat weight, which means the hull, motor, batteries, fuel, gear and every person aboard. Bass Pro's guidance is slightly more generous at 5 lb of thrust per 200 lb, or 2.5 lb per 100 lb. Treat either as a minimum, not a target.
          </p>

          <p>
            A worked example makes it concrete. A 14 ft aluminum jon boat might weigh around 200 lb, and with two anglers at 400 lb, a 50 lb battery, a small outboard and gear, the loaded total can easily reach 900 to 1,000 lb. At 2 lb per 100 lb, that boat needs at least 18 to 20 lb of thrust, so a 55 lb motor has plenty in reserve. A 2,000 lb loaded bass boat needs at least 40 lb by the rule, and in practice most owners go well above it for wind.
          </p>

          <p>
            Go up a size if you fish in wind, current, tides or open water, or if you want the motor to hold position rather than just creep along. The extra thrust costs little in battery use at low speed settings, because a variable-speed motor only draws what the speed setting asks for. Going under the rule is where buyers regret it: an underpowered motor runs flat out all day, drains the battery faster and still loses the fight against a crosswind.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE OF THUMB</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">At least 2 lb of thrust per 100 lb of loaded boat weight</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Count the hull, batteries, gear and people. Add more if you fish wind, current or tides. Bass Pro's figure is 2.5 lb per 100 lb, which is a safer starting point for open water.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Our 6 Picks
          </h2>

          <p>
            We researched these from manufacturer specifications, retailer listings and published reviews, and we note where sources disagree. We did not lab-test them. Thrust, amp draw and shaft lengths are the listed figures; real speed and run time depend on your boat, battery, wind and current.
          </p>

